1. The DNS Request: Finding the Address
The first step is translation. Computers don't understand "https://www.google.com", they understand IP addresses (like 142.250.190.46).

Browser Cache: Your browser checks if it already knows the IP.
OS Cache & Resolver: If not, it asks your operating system, then your Internet Service Provider (ISP).
DNS Hierarchy: A request is sent to DNS servers to find the "phone number" of the server.

2. TCP/IP: Connecting the Dots
Once the IP is found, your browser needs to establish a connection.

TCP 3-way Handshake: The client (you) and the server exchange three packets (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK) to ensure they are both ready to talk.
IP Protocol: This ensures the packets are routed correctly across the internet.

3. Firewall & HTTPS: The Security Gate
Firewall: The request must pass through firewalls on both sides to ensure the traffic is allowed.
SSL/TLS Handshake: Since it's HTTPS (port 443), your browser and the server perform a "handshake" to encrypt the communication using SSL certificates. No one can spy on your search!

4. The Load Balancer: Distributing the Load
Google receives billions of requests. A single server would explode.

The request hits a Load Balancer (like the HAproxy we discussed earlier).
It chooses the "healthiest" or closest available server to handle your request.

5. The Web & Application Servers: Processing
Web Server (Nginx/Apache): It receives the request and decides what to do.
Application Server: It runs the code (Python, Go, Java) to generate the page based on who you are and what you searched for.
Database: The application queries a database to get your profile, search history, etc.

6. The Rendering: Bringing the Page to Life
The server sends back an HTTP Response (usually with a 200 OK status code).

Your browser receives HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.
The DOM: The browser parses the HTML to build the structure.
Rendering: It applies CSS and executes JS to display the Google search bar you know.